WEBVTT

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:05.754
Closing deals takes finding deals.

00:00:05.774 --> 00:00:07.543
But most new-logo reps never

00:00:07.563 --> 00:00:09.163
get the first meeting.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.069
Most prospecting emails get ignored because

00:00:12.089 --> 00:00:13.624
they lead with the product.

00:00:13.644 --> 00:00:15.940
What gets a reply is a short note:

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.362
real homework, real proof, and a small ask.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.410
Meet Kelsey Dalton.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:22.660
Over 10 years in

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.731
enterprise technology sales,

00:00:24.751 --> 00:00:26.949
she rose from account executive to leading

00:00:26.969 --> 00:00:29.866
new-logo teams as a Regional Sales Director

00:00:29.886 --> 00:00:31.814
and a Regional Vice President.

00:00:31.834 --> 00:00:33.934
Today she's home with her two kids,

00:00:33.954 --> 00:00:35.344
but her passion for helping

00:00:35.364 --> 00:00:37.491
sellers grow hasn't changed.

00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:41.139
She built a company's first SMB sales team,

00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:43.026
was named Manager of the Year,

00:00:43.046 --> 00:00:44.693
and saw three of her reps reach

00:00:44.713 --> 00:00:46.997
President's Club in a single year,

00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:49.426
with five more promoted to enterprise.

00:00:50.217 --> 00:00:53.077
So Kelsey turned her system into a playbook:

00:00:53.097 --> 00:00:54.939
The Repeat Success Method.

00:00:54.959 --> 00:00:56.127
Four parts.

00:00:56.147 --> 00:00:58.716
One repeatable way to land new logos.

00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:01.270
It's built for newer account executives

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.758
and BDRs who need first meetings,

00:01:03.778 --> 00:01:05.743
for managers coaching newer reps,

00:01:05.763 --> 00:01:07.238
and for founders standing up

00:01:07.258 --> 00:01:09.177
their first sales team.

00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:11.670
Part one: Prioritize.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.765
Score your accounts into four tiers,

00:01:13.785 --> 00:01:16.204
and spend your best hours on Tier One.

00:01:16.224 --> 00:01:18.386
The best accounts aren't the biggest.

00:01:18.406 --> 00:01:19.783
They're the ones that changed:

00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:22.172
new ownership, a funding round,

00:01:22.192 --> 00:01:25.484
a new leader, or a deal that died last year.

00:01:25.883 --> 00:01:27.529
Part two: People.

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:28.901
Follow the money.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:31.254
Sales and finance usually own it,

00:01:31.274 --> 00:01:32.553
so know who you're selling to

00:01:32.573 --> 00:01:34.122
and what they care about.

00:01:34.142 --> 00:01:35.838
Start closest to the pain,

00:01:35.858 --> 00:01:37.654
and earn your way up.

00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:39.690
Part three: Proof.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.202
Every first email has three parts.

00:01:42.222 --> 00:01:44.923
In this made-up example, the reason:

00:01:44.943 --> 00:01:47.202
a second warehouse just opened.

00:01:47.222 --> 00:01:48.317
The proof:

00:01:48.337 --> 00:01:50.719
a similar company cut missed deliveries from

00:01:50.739 --> 00:01:52.729
one in five to one in twenty.

00:01:52.749 --> 00:01:55.538
The ask: 15 to 20 minutes.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.428
And one rule matters most:

00:01:58.448 --> 00:01:59.946
never invent a number.

00:01:59.966 --> 00:02:02.621
Use only results you're approved to share.

00:02:03.283 --> 00:02:05.665
Each step comes with AI prompts:

00:02:05.685 --> 00:02:07.810
find your reason, write the draft,

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.144
then cut it down.

00:02:09.164 --> 00:02:10.590
And keep it safe.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.742
Customer names,

00:02:11.762 --> 00:02:13.948
contact details and pricing stay

00:02:13.968 --> 00:02:15.471
out of AI tools.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.088
Part four: Persistence.

00:02:18.108 --> 00:02:20.073
Don't send "just checking in."

00:02:20.093 --> 00:02:22.132
Give them something new every time:

00:02:22.152 --> 00:02:25.012
a one-page before and after, a short video,

00:02:25.032 --> 00:02:26.914
or news from their company.

00:02:27.417 --> 00:02:29.651
Before you send, five checks.

00:02:29.671 --> 00:02:31.073
A real reason.

00:02:31.093 --> 00:02:32.849
An approved proof point.

00:02:32.869 --> 00:02:34.994
A 15 to 20 minute ask.

00:02:35.014 --> 00:02:36.575
Short enough for a phone.

00:02:36.595 --> 00:02:39.323
And every fact checked, in your own voice.

00:02:39.783 --> 00:02:42.128
The full playbook brings it all together:

00:02:42.148 --> 00:02:44.297
templates, your proof library,

00:02:44.317 --> 00:02:46.748
the outreach cadence, the first meeting,

00:02:46.768 --> 00:02:49.296
and AI prompts for every step.

00:02:49.316 --> 00:02:50.889
Plus a scoring toolkit,

00:02:50.909 --> 00:02:53.074
and a bonus guide for managers.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.262
Start with the free guide today,

00:02:55.282 --> 00:02:56.316
and be first to know

00:02:56.336 --> 00:02:57.810
when the playbook opens.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.709
Root and Reinvent.

00:02:59.729 --> 00:03:00.812
You've built it.

00:03:00.832 --> 00:03:02.543
Now grow it.
